Hey Kamal,

Thanks for your response.

Here is my response to your comment about:

> 1. We could start producing keyed records to the __remote_log_metadata
>    topic and optional tombstone records based on the metadata version,
> while
>    leaving the existing topic configuration unchanged. For example:
>    metadataCache.metadataVersion().isRemoteLogTombstoneRecordsSupported()

I have introduced a metadata `remote.log.metadata.version` to track the
progress of the upgrade, and it has 3 values,
Version 0: Current state (delete-only, may contain null-key messages)
Version 1: Transition state (compact+delete, allows null-key messages to
expire via retention while preventing compaction)
Version 2: Final state (compact-only, all messages have keys).
But tombstone records can be emitted once the broker starts to run the new
code, otherwise it would generate a lot of tombstone messages once the
feature is fully turned on. And actually emitting tombstone records at the
very first has no side-effect, the keyed records will not be deleted before
the upgrade finished since the min.compaction.lag.ms is set the same as the
retention.ms[1], the actual compaction of keyed message will only happen
when the metadata's value is set as 2.


>    2. We can provide a verification script that checks whether all record=
s
>    in the metadata topic have non-null keys before changing the topic
>    configuration. Once verified, users can update the topic's cleanup
> policy
>    and other compaction-related configurations using kafka-configs.sh. We
>    should document this process in the upgrade notes.

I'm glad that we share the same opinion and the upgrade path is built the
same as what you described above, and the whole upgrade path is documented
in this note [2]. You can find the description to the full upgrade path in
this note. But this is a preview: we use a script
kafka-remote-log-metadata-migration.sh  to help the user upgrade, and this
script will help verify whether all null-key messages have been deleted
prior to set the metadata topic as compact.

   3. Newly created __remote_log_metadata topics can be configured with the
>    cleanup policy set to compact.


This is already how the new code will behave, see code [3].

We could also consider removing topicName
> from the key and using KRaftMetadataCache to resolve the topic name from
> the topicId when needed. That said, keeping topicName in the key is also
> reasonable if the overhead is only a few extra bytes and avoids an
> additional lookup into KRaftMetadataCache.

I prefer not adding more dependency in TopicBasedRemoteLogMetadataManager
than what we already have, and I believe most of the time, the topic name
is of reasonable length, and also, the memory usage it would increase is as
value in this map endOffsetToSegments, this map will only keep the active
used remote log segments' metadata, and the entries will be deleted once
the tombstone message is published. That said, if this becomes a
bottleneck, I can make another proposal to mitigate this issue.

