Hi Luke, Federico, Omnia, and the whole team,

Before anything else - thank you. We have been iterating on this KIP for
months now, and I deeply appreciate how thoroughly and seriously you have
engaged with every architectural and security concern raised throughout
this review process. Digging into each edge case, updating the design
specification, and opening the tracking JIRAs has ensured a robust design
for a change of this scale.


*VK12 (Data-Safety Clamp): Confirmed Closed.*I see the defensive clamp
fully integrated into both the MirrorMetadataManager section and the Group
Offsets section:
max(dest log start offset, min(dest LEO, source committed offset))
The inclusion of the worked execution example completely resolves my
data-safety concerns regarding offset boundaries. Thank you for folding
this critical validation layer into the core design.


*VK10 (Group-Namespace Validation & KAFKA-20799): Resolved.*Your reasoning
is structurally sound, and I verified the behavior against the core
implementation to be certain. Because group names are unique across
consumer/share/streams types by design (with GroupMetadataManager throwing
a GroupIdNotFoundException for cross-type access) and synced offset commits
route through the standard OffsetCommit path, an isolated pre-flight gate
in mirroring would be incomplete.

The correct structural fix belongs at the group-namespace level in core
Kafka. I am completely aligned with dropping the localized gate framework;
KAFKA-20799 is precisely the right architectural home for this ecosystem
fix.

To assist with the implementation of KAFKA-20799, here is how I view the
priority structure for production readiness (non-blocking for this KIP):

*Actionable Error Telemetry:* Enhance the exception payload to explicitly
include the colliding group name, its type in each cluster, and the
associated mirror name, rather than suppressing it behind a bare
GroupIdNotFoundException. This transforms an ambiguous failover-time skip
into a self-explaining, actionable diagnostic event.

*Operational Metrics: *Introduce a dedicated metric counter tracking
group-offset-sync collisions/errors so platform operators can alert on
collisions deterministically instead of parsing distributed logs.

*On-Demand Diagnostics (Lowest Priority):* Expose an on-demand
describe/list capability to surface point-in-time name collisions, which is
highly valuable for identifying pre-existing conflicts during initial
environment setup.

Item (1) delivers the primary architectural value; the remaining items can
land according to your core roadmap. I would be glad to serve as the
reviewer for the KAFKA-20799 PR once it is ready for code review.

With VK12 verified and VK10 properly decoupled and tracked in KAFKA-20799,
I have no remaining architectural concerns. You have my full endorsement on
KIP-1279. This is a highly significant advancement for Kafka's distributed
mirroring capabilities, executed with excellent rigor.

Outstanding work by the entire team.

Regards,
Vaquar Khan
