Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-20803) Share-group persister: O(N^2)
 response demultiplexing in PersisterStateManager batched RPCs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "Shekhar Prasad Rajak (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

Shekhar Prasad Rajak created KAFKA-20803:
--------------------------------------------

             Summary: Share-group persister: O(N^2) response demultiplexing=
 in PersisterStateManager batched RPCs
                 Key: KAFKA-20803
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-20803
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Improvement
    Affects Versions: 4.3.1
            Reporter: Shekhar Prasad Rajak
             Fix For: 4.3.2


When the share-group persister coalesces many partitions into a single WRIT=
E/READ/READ_SUMMARY/INITIALIZE/DELETE_SHARE_GROUP_STATE RPC, the response i=
s demultiplexed in O(N ^ 2) time, where N is the number of
=C2=A0 partitions batched into that RPC. This runs on the network send thre=
ad, so it also stalls unrelated in-flight RPCs.

=C2=A0

*Current behavior*

=C2=A0 Request coalescing is fine =E2=80=94 RequestCoalescerHelper.coalesce=
Writes (PersisterStateManager.java) folds all N per-partition handlers into=
 one request in a single O(N) pass.

=C2=A0 The response side is the problem. The completion callback hands the =
entire combined response to every handler:

=C2=A0 // PersisterStateManager.generateRequests()
=C2=A0 handlersPerGroup.forEach(handler1 -> handler1.onComplete(response));

=C2=A0 Each handler then linearly scans the full response to locate its own=
 single PartitionResult:

=C2=A0 // WriteStateHandler.handleRequestResponse(),
=C2=A0 for (WriteStateResult r : combinedResponse.data().results()) =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0// walk all topics
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 if (r.topicId().equals(partitionKey().topicId()))
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 r.partitions().stream()
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0.filter(p -> p.partition() =3D=3D =
partitionKey().partition()) =C2=A0// walk all partitions
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0.findFirst();

=C2=A0 N handlers =C3=97 O(N) scan each =3D O(N ^ 2). The five handler type=
s (WriteStateHandler, ReadStateHandler, ReadStateSummaryHandler, Initialize=
StateHandler, DeleteStateHandler) all share this=C2=A0 identical pattern.

=C2=A0

=C2=A0

*Why it matters*
=C2=A0=C2=A0
=C2=A0 - Share groups typically subscribe to a topic with many partitions, =
and many partitions hash to the same share-coordinator node, so N is large =
in practice (hundreds).
=C2=A0 - The demux executes inside the completion callback on the NetworkCl=
ient/InterBrokerSendThread, so the O(N^2) work blocks the send loop and del=
ays every other queued RPC (reads, writes to other coordinators,
=C2=A0 find-coordinator). wakeup()=C2=A0 only fires after the scan complete=
s.

=C2=A0

=C2=A0*Proposed fix*
=C2=A0=C2=A0
=C2=A0 Demultiplex the combined response once into a Map<Uuid topicId, Map<=
Integer partition, PartitionResult>> (O(N)), then hand each handler only it=
s own slice for an O(1) lookup =E2=80=94 replacing the per-handler linear
=C2=A0 scan.

=C2=A0



